ions, remains the dominant driver of rate-hike odds for the remainder of 2026. The FOMC held the federal funds rate at 3.50%-3.75% in its July 28-29 meeting, with three dissents favoring a hike, while July's softer jobs report tempered near-term expectations. Market-implied pricing via fed funds futures now assigns roughly 30-65% odds of a 25-basis-point increase by the September 15-16 meeting, though a Reuters poll of economists shows about 80% anticipating no change through year-end. Key upcoming catalysts include the August CPI release, September FOMC decision, and any further labor-market data that could shift the balance between growth concerns and price stability.","context_requires_regen":false,"context_updated_at":"2026-08-20T12:46:32.909Z"},"version":"v1"}]
